## 3. The room server (Cloudflare Workers + Durable Objects)

### 3.1 Layout and tooling

- New top-level folder **`server/`**, its own small project: `server/package.json` (dev dependency `wrangler` only), `server/wrangler.toml`, `server/src/index.js` and `server/README.md`. The game's "no npm dependencies" rule applies to the game. The server is a separate deployable, like `tools/`. Say this in `ALVA_SPEC.md` §2 and §10.
- **Exclude `server/` from the itch release.** Add it to the excluded paths in `tools/itch/validate.mjs` / `release.mjs` (next to `tools`, `docs`, `tests`) and make sure the build's file count doesn't change.
- Plain JavaScript (ES modules), matching the repo's style. Import the shared `js/online/protocol.js` validators (bundled by wrangler).
- Use **one Durable Object class, `Lobby`, with a single instance** (`idFromName('global')`). It holds the room directory, enforces the caps and relays signaling for every room. At this scale one instance is plenty, and it's the simplest way to enforce a global cap. Use the **SQLite-backed** Durable Object class (`new_sqlite_classes` in the `migrations` of `wrangler.toml`), which is what the Workers Free plan supports.
- Use the **WebSocket Hibernation API** (`state.acceptWebSocket`, `webSocketMessage`, `webSocketClose`, socket attachments via `serializeAttachment`) so idle sockets don't bill duration. Keep room state in the DO's storage or attachments so it survives hibernation.

### 3.2 Protocol (JSON over one WebSocket per client)

Client → server: `hello {name, protocolVersion}`, `listRooms` (subscribes to public list pushes), `createRoom {roomName, visibility, mode}`, `joinRoom {code}`, `updateRoom {roomName?, visibility?}` (host only), `leaveRoom`, `signal {to, data}` (SDP/ICE relay, only to the other member of your own room), `startMatch` / `matchEnded` (room status), `ping`.

Server → client: `welcome {clientId}`, `rooms [{code, roomName, hostName, players, mode}]` (public, open rooms only), `roomJoined {room, role}`, `roomUpdated {room}`, `peerJoined {name}`, `peerLeft {reason}`, `signal {from, data}`, `error {code}` with codes `SERVERS_FULL`, `ROOM_NOT_FOUND`, `ROOM_FULL`, `BAD_NAME`, `BAD_ROOM_NAME`, `RATE_LIMITED`, `VERSION_MISMATCH`, `pong`.

- Validate every message's shape and size (reject > 16 KB, drop unknown types). Validate names with the same rules as the client.
- Room codes: 6 characters from `ABCDEFGHJKMNPQRSTUVWXYZ23456789`, unique among open rooms, generated with `crypto.getRandomValues`.
- A room holds max 2 players. It closes when its host leaves, when it's empty, or after **30 minutes** without a match starting (DO alarm).
- **Origin check** on the WebSocket upgrade, against an allowlist from a `wrangler.toml` var: `ALLOWED_ORIGINS`. In PR 1 include `http://localhost:*` and `http://127.0.0.1:*` for development, `https://html-classic.itch.zone` (where itch runs HTML5 games, in an iframe) and `https://hiyroscript.github.io`. Confirm the itch origin in PR 2 by testing; itch also serves some games from `https://*.ssl.hwcdn.net`, so check.
- `GET /health` returns `{ok, rooms, capacity}` for the smoke test.
- Per-connection rate limit (for example 20 messages per 10 s outside `signal`, 200 for `signal` during setup), then `RATE_LIMITED` and close.
- Log no personal data. Names live only in memory and the room's lifetime.

### 3.3 Deployment

- Add **`.github/workflows/deploy-room-server.yml`**, `workflow_dispatch` only (a manual "Run workflow" button). It installs `server/` and runs `wrangler deploy` with `CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN` and `CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID` from repository secrets, then prints the deployed `*.workers.dev` URL in the job summary. This way the owner never needs a terminal. Also document the local path (`cd server && npx wrangler login && npx wrangler deploy`) as an alternative.
- `npm run dev` in `server/` runs `wrangler dev` locally for development.

### 3.4 Staying inside the free plan ("Servers are full")

Cloudflare's Workers Free plan allows Durable Objects **100,000 requests per day** (a WebSocket connection counts as a request; incoming WebSocket messages count at 20:1; outgoing messages are free) and 13,000 GB-s of duration per day. Going over makes further requests fail until 00:00 UTC rather than billing. Check the current numbers on https://developers.cloudflare.com/durable-objects/platform/pricing before you finalize. We never want to hit those limits, so:

- **Concurrent room cap:** `MAX_ROOMS` (default **40**) and `MAX_CONNECTIONS` (default **120**) as `wrangler.toml` vars. `createRoom` past the cap → `SERVERS_FULL`. A new connection past the connection cap → `SERVERS_FULL` and close.
- **Daily budget guard:** the DO counts billable units per UTC day in storage (connections + incoming messages ÷ 20 + HTTP requests). Past **`DAILY_BUDGET`** (default **70,000**, a 30% safety margin) it refuses new rooms and connections with `SERVERS_FULL` until the UTC day rolls over. Existing matches continue.
- Keep traffic low by design: list updates are pushed (never polled), lobby sockets that sit idle for **5 minutes** are closed by the server (the client reconnects when the player acts), and the in-room heartbeat is ≤ 1 message per 20 s. Game input never touches the server.
- In `server/README.md`, write a short budget estimate per match (connections plus messages) and how many matches per day the defaults allow.

